        </div>
        <div class="ms-md-4">
          <div class="d-flex flex-column flex-md-row justify-content-between">
            <div>
              <strong>OMIX: Offline Mixing for Self-Tallying Elections</strong>
              <div class="mt-1"><span class="fst-italic">Sébastien Canard, Liam Medley, Duy Nguyen, Duong Hieu Phan</span></div>
            </div>
            
            <div class="float-end mt-1 ms-md-3">
              <small class="badge category category-PROTOCOLS">Cryptographic protocols</small>
            </div>
            
          </div>
          <p class="mb-0 mt-1 search-abstract">In e-voting, a mix-net is a widely used system for shuffling votes in order to guarantee voter privacy. The core principle is as follows: (1) N voters cast their votes; (2) a sequence of mix-servers shuffles the N encrypted ballots, thereby randomly permuting the underlying votes; and (3) trustees jointly decrypt the shuffled ballots to compute the tally.

This approach, however, relies heavily on trusted parties after the voting phase and raises serious robustness issues. For example,...</p>
        </div>

      </div>
      
      <div class="mb-4">
        <div class="d-flex"><a title="2025/1017" class="paperlink" href="/2025/1017">2025/1017</a>
